39,1047,1048],{},"whatis grep",[59,1050,1052],{"id":1051},"manual-sections","Manual Sections",[14,1054,1055,1056,1059,1060,1063],{},"Man pages are grouped into sections; use ",[39,1057,1058],{},"man 5 passwd"," (file format) vs ",[39,1061,1062],{},"man 1 passwd"," (command):",[202,1065,1066,1076],{},[205,1067,1068],{},[208,1069,1070,1073],{},[211,1071,1072],{},"Section",[211,1074,1075],{},"Content",[224,1077,1078,1086,1094,1102,1110,1118,1126,1134],{},[208,1079,1080,1083],{},[229,1081,1082],{},"1",[229,1084,1085],{},"User commands",[208,1087,1088,1091],{},[229,1089,1090],{},"2",[229,1092,1093],{},"System calls",[208,1095,1096,1099],{},[229,1097,1098],{},"3",[229,1100,1101],{},"Library functions",[208,1103,1104,1107],{},[229,1105,1106],{},"4",[229,1108,1109],{},"Special files (devices)",[208,1111,1112,1115],{},[229,1113,1114],{},"5",[229,1116,1117],{},"File formats & config",[208,1119,1120,1123],{},[229,1121,1122],{},"6",[229,1124,1125],{},"Games",[208,1127,1128,1131],{},[229,1129,1130],{},"7",[229,1132,1133],{},"Conventions, misc",[208,1135,1136,1139],{},[229,1137,1138],{},"8",[229,1140,1141],{},"System administration",[108,1143,1144],{"language":48},[64,1145,1147],{"className":112,"code":1146,"language":48,"meta":70,"style":70},"man 5 crontab     # the crontab FILE format, not the command\nman 2 open        # the open(2) SYSCALL, not any command\nman -k cron       # search for \"cron\" across all sections\n",[39,1148,1149,1163,1176],{"__ignoreMap":70},[117,1150,1151,1154,1157,1160],{"class":119,"line":120},[117,1152,1153],{"class":123},"man",[117,1155,1156],{"class":127}," 5",[117,1158,1159],{"class":131}," crontab",[117,1161,1162],{"class":148},"     # the crontab FILE format, not the command\n",[117,1164,1165,1167,1170,1173],{"class":119,"line":364},[117,1166,1153],{"class":123},[117,1168,1169],{"class":127}," 2",[117,1171,1172],{"class":131}," open",[117,1174,1175],{"class":148},"        # the open(2) SYSCALL, not any command\n",[117,1177,1178,1180,1183,1186],{"class":119,"line":373},[117,1179,1153],{"class":123},[117,1181,1182],{"class":127}," -k",[117,1184,1185],{"class":131}," cron",[117,1187,1188],{"class":148},"       # search for \"cron\" across all sections\n",[27,1190,1192],{"id":1191},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[74,1194,1195,1215,1225,1243,1255],{},[77,1196,1197,1200,1201,1204,1205,1207,1208,1211,1212,1214],{},[18,1198,1199],{},"Idiom",": use ",[39,1202,1203],{},"cat \u002Fetc\u002Fos-release"," (not ",[39,1206,388],{},") for portable distro detection — ",[39,1209,1210],{},"os-release"," is specified by the FHS and present on every modern distro; ",[39,1213,388],{}," is missing on minimal installs.",[77,1216,1217,1200,1219,1221,1222,57],{},[18,1218,1199],{},[39,1220,367],{}," on systemd distros — it shows distro, kernel, arch, hostname, and chassis type in one command. Falls back to ",[39,1223,1224],{},"cat \u002Fetc\u002Fos-release && uname -a",[77,1226,1227,1200,1229,1204,1232,1235,1236,1239,1240,1242],{},[18,1228,1199],{},[39,1230,1231],{},"command -v \u003Cname>",[39,1233,1234],{},"which",") to check if a command exists — ",[39,1237,1238],{},"command -v"," is POSIX, built into the shell, and respects functions\u002Faliases. ",[39,1241,1234],{}," is an external command and misses shell functions.",[77,1244,1245,1247,1248,1251,1252,1254],{},[18,1246,1199],{},": bookmark ",[39,1249,1250],{},"man 7 hier"," — it documents the FHS in man-page form. Run ",[39,1253,1250],{}," for the authoritative description of what each top-level directory is for.",[77,1256,1257,1200,1260,1263,1264,42,1266,42,1268,1270,1271,1273,1274,1276],{},[18,1258,1259],{},"Debug",[39,1261,1262],{},"strace -c \u003Ccmd>"," to see which syscalls a command makes — reveals what \"everything is a file\" really means (",[39,1265,166],{},[39,1267,170],{},[39,1269,177],{}," on ",[39,1272,665],{}," and ",[39,1275,675],{}," paths).",[27,1278,1280],{"id":1279},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[74,1282,1283,1311,1329,1349,1365,1391,1418],{},[77,1284,1285,1292,1293,1273,1296,1299,1300,1303,1304,1307,1308,57],{},[18,1286,1287,1273,1289,1291],{},[39,1288,568],{},[39,1290,588],{}," are symlinks on modern distros",": since Debian\u002FUbuntu \"merged \u002Fusr\", ",[39,1294,1295],{},"\u002Fbin → \u002Fusr\u002Fbin",[39,1297,1298],{},"\u002Fsbin → \u002Fusr\u002Fsbin",". Scripts hardcoding ",[39,1301,1302],{},"\u002Fbin\u002Ffoo"," still work, but the real path is ",[39,1305,1306],{},"\u002Fusr\u002Fbin\u002Ffoo",". Check with ",[39,1309,1310],{},"ls -ld \u002Fbin",[77,1312,1313,1320,1321,1324,1325,1328],{},[18,1314,1315,1273,1317,1319],{},[39,1316,665],{},[39,1318,675],{}," are virtual",": they exist in RAM, not on disk. ",[39,1322,1323],{},"du -sh \u002Fproc"," reports 0 or errors. Don't back them up. Writing to ",[39,1326,1327],{},"\u002Fproc\u002Fsys"," changes live kernel state — typos can crash the system.",[77,1330,1331,1336,1337,1340,1341,1344,1345,1348],{},[18,1332,1333,1335],{},[39,1334,142],{}," is not a regular file",": it's a character device. ",[39,1338,1339],{},"cp realfile \u002Fdev\u002Fnull"," doesn't \"save\" the file — it discards it. ",[39,1342,1343],{},"cat \u002Fdev\u002Fnull > bigfile"," truncates ",[39,1346,1347],{},"bigfile"," to 0 bytes (a common idiom).",[77,1350,1351,1356,1357,1360,1361,1364],{},[18,1352,1353,1355],{},[39,1354,655],{}," is cleared on reboot"," (and sometimes hourly by ",[39,1358,1359],{},"systemd-tmpfiles","): don't store anything you need to keep. Use ",[39,1362,1363],{},"\u002Fvar\u002Ftmp"," for files that survive reboots (up to 30 days by default).",[77,1366,1367,1373,1374,1377,1378,42,1380,1382,1383,1386,1387,1390],{},[18,1368,1369,1372],{},[39,1370,1371],{},"$SHELL"," is your login shell, not necessarily your current shell",": if you run ",[39,1375,1376],{},"zsh"," from ",[39,1379,48],{},[39,1381,1371],{}," still says ",[39,1384,1385],{},"\u002Fbin\u002Fbash",". Use ",[39,1388,1389],{},"ps -p $$ -o comm="," for the current shell.",[77,1392,1393,1402,1403,1406,1407,1409,1410,1413,1414,1417],{},[18,1394,1395,1398,1399],{},[39,1396,1397],{},"uname -r"," vs ",[39,1400,1401],{},"uname -a",": ",[39,1404,1405],{},"-r"," gives only the kernel release (",[39,1408,417],{},"), which is what ",[39,1411,1412],{},"apt install linux-image-$(uname -r)"," expects. ",[39,1415,1416],{},"-a"," includes hostname, kernel name, etc.",[77,1419,1420,1423,1424,1427],{},[18,1421,1422],{},"Alpine uses musl, not glibc",": binaries compiled against glibc (most prebuilt Linux binaries) won't run on Alpine without ",[39,1425,1426],{},"gcompat"," or a musl build. This bites Docker users pulling glibc binaries into Alpine images.",[27,1429,1431],{"id":1430},"quick-quiz","🧠 Quick Quiz",[14,1433,1434,1435,1438,1439,1442],{},"You run ",[39,1436,1437],{},"man passwd"," and see the command documentation. You actually wanted the format of ",[39,1440,1441],{},"\u002Fetc\u002Fpasswd",". What went wrong, and how do you fix it?",[1444,1445,1446,1450,1470,1486],"details",{},[1447,1448,1449],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1451,1452,1454,1455,1458,1459,1463,1464,1458,1466,1469],{},[39,1453,1437],{}," defaults to the lowest-numbered section with a match — section 1 (user commands), the ",[39,1456,1457],{},"passwd"," ",[1460,1461,1462],"em",{},"command",". The ",[39,1465,1441],{},[1460,1467,1468],{},"file format"," is in section 5. Fix it with:",[108,1471,1472],{"language":48},[64,1473,1475],{"className":112,"code":1474,"language":48,"meta":70,"style":70},"man 5 passwd\n",[39,1476,1477],{"__ignoreMap":70},[117,1478,1479,1481,1483],{"class":119,"line":120},[117,1480,1153],{"class":123},[117,1482,1156],{"class":127},[117,1484,1485],{"class":131}," passwd\n",[14,1487,1488,1489,1492,1493,1496,1497,1500,1501,1504,1505,1508,1509,1512,1513,1516,1517,1520],{},"This is the section-number syntax: ",[39,1490,1491],{},"man \u003Csection> \u003Cname>",". When a name exists in multiple sections, always specify the section to avoid ambiguity. Other common pairs: ",[39,1494,1495],{},"man 1 crontab"," (command) vs ",[39,1498,1499],{},"man 5 crontab"," (file), ",[39,1502,1503],{},"man 2 open"," (syscall) vs ",[39,1506,1507],{},"man 1 open"," (if a command named ",[39,1510,1511],{},"open"," exists), ",[39,1514,1515],{},"man 8 mount"," (admin command) vs ",[39,1518,1519],{},"man 2 mount"," (syscall).",[1522,1523,1524],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .sIsaT, html code.shiki .sIsaT{--shiki-default:#6F42C1;--shiki-github-dark:#B392F0}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html.github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}",{"title":70,"searchDepth":364,"depth":364,"links":1526},[1527,1530,1531,1534,1535,1536,1537,1538,1541,1542,1543],{"id":29,"depth":364,"text":30,"children":1528},[1529],{"id":61,"depth":373,"text":62},{"id":94,"depth":364,"text":95},{"id":196,"depth":364,"text":197,"children":1532},[1533],{"id":312,"depth":373,"text":313},{"id":342,"depth":364,"text":343},{"id":435,"depth":364,"text":436},{"id":540,"depth":364,"text":541},{"id":726,"depth":364,"text":727},{"id":955,"depth":364,"text":956,"children":1539},[1540],{"id":1051,"depth":373,"text":1052},{"id":1191,"depth":364,"text":1192},{"id":1279,"depth":364,"text":1280},{"id":1430,"depth":364,"text":1431},"Linux is a free, open-source, Unix-like operating system kernel. When combined with GNU userspace tools, a display server, and applications, it forms a complete operating system called a Linux distribution (or \"distro\").","md",{},true,"\u002Flinux\u002F01-introduction-and-architecture",{"title":5,"description":1544},"linux\u002F01-introduction-and-architecture","OB-ylNYoMRr3j_3Hm92K2D3UTvzg4ecA1nPAlvuLsCE",1787401629645]